- I think the information on the top right of the lattice website is working against it. And the 'fixed points' bit is confusing; get rid of it.
- Please get rid of my description (both texts, above and below the name). I want to be very deliberate about that, and to integrate the resulting text better into the lattice effect. Let's start with just cryptic headlines. Maybe 'mathematics', 'point cloud research', 'scientific programming', 'American pool', 'digital design', 'self-help'. The current lattice behavior is just gibberish, but it doesn't have to. Please try your best to make those words emerge from that gibberish, as if by chance. Unless you have a better approach, I think a good start would be to have the words in the phrase lay hidden until a head crashes with it, and then make the word visible from the impact point outwards as if the head just split because of the impact. Instead of the disappearing trail after a head, the word should become visible for a couple of seconds, and then fade. Once it is completely face the next phrase should be put in place, hidden again, until a head finds it. The phrases should rotate in a cycle.
- The Russell's quote I didn't mean as such a central element of the page, but a subtle souvenir. Can you hide it usign a similar mechanism? The quote is too big to fit in the website with the spacing the lattice gives, but I think having it show like a news ticker in a portion of the page could do the trick!
- I asked you to keep the threads of light connecting the bb logo to the nodes. Rather than just a mostly static light thread like in the armillary site, and rather than having those circles of light in the lattice site, let's make the links activate when a head impacts on them. They should absorb the stream (just to make sure; I'm using 'head' to refer to the initial element of the moving 'streams' within the lattice) and light up the connection through a pulse in both directions.
- The signed should be modified to contain only three rings. Each ring should be connected to one of the three axis inherent to the hexagonal structure (in the current lattice website 1g49 and LitS form one, CB Teo and firecoat.me another, and cv and penquire the third one). A ring has to degrees of freedom for movement, and each one should be connected to one link. When a head crashes into a link, it is absorbed by it, and generates a pulse of light towards the correspondent node, and another towards the signet. When the pulse of light reaches the signet, the corresponding ring should be given a bump, in the direction of movement that that link is connected to. So, for short, the three rings have a total of 6 degrees of freedom for movement, each connected to a link, in such a way that opposing links activate the same ring, only in different directions of movement. Rings should loose speed over time, calibrated to compensate the expected number of pulses received.
